  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 234 PM MDT Sat Aug 15 2026\n\nAn active start to the weekend continues across Eastern Idaho \nas residual mid-level moisture fuels scattered afternoon showers\nand thunderstorms. The primary operational focus remains on the\nWapiti Burn Scar; however, rainfall rates across the scar have \nremained light to moderate thus far. As moisture transport \nshifts eastward, the Flash Flood Watch for the Wapiti scar will \nbe allowed to expire later this evening. A noticeable drying \ntrend takes hold for the second half of the weekend. While high-\nresolution convective-allowing models (CAMs) keep scattered \nshowers (30\u201340% PoPs) focused over the Central Mountains on \nSunday, shower potential across the Snake Plain remains low- \nconfidence and highly isolated. Increased sunshine will allow \nlower-elevation maximum temperatures to rebound back toward \nseasonal benchmarks in the mid-to-upper 80s.\n\nA quick-moving upper-level trough will track across the Pacific\nNorthwest into southern Canada Monday into Tuesday. While the \ncore dynamics of this feature remain well north of the Idaho \nborder, associated height falls and weak shortwave energy will \nclip the northern tier of our forecast area. This will re- \nintroduce a 30% to 50% probability of showers and thunderstorms \nacross the Central Mountains, the Montana Divide, and into the \nEastern Highlands. Given the drier low-level air mass in place, \nstorms in these zones will be capable of gusty outflow winds \nalongside localized brief downpours. Further south across the \nSnake Plain and Magic Valley, precipitation chances drop below \n15%. \n\nBehind the departing northern disturbance, global model\nensembles display strong consensus in rebuilding a robust, high-\namplitude ridge of high pressure over the Great Basin. As 500mb\nheights rise, a warm, dry continental air mass will lock into\nplace across the Intermountain West. This setup will effectively\nshut off monsoonal moisture transport, returning Eastern Idaho\nto a classic late-summer weather pattern. Expect widespread\nsunshine, low relative humidity, and a substantial warming trend\nthrough mid-to-late week. Afternoon temperatures will steadily\nclimb back into the 90s across valley locations, running 5 to 10\ndegrees above seasonal climate normals heading into next\nweekend.",
